      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{5b07764455c49e65e1c52311|full_name}: I respectfully disagree. Sure, in Avatar 2.0, we can build many *digital* control tools, and yes we can use FinalIK to control object position, and yes we can use shaders.\nThe idea of Avatar 3.0 is to allow driving things through the main animator, so there's something compelling about having a set or puppet parameters. For example, driving a 2d blendtree of blend shapes, or controlling a wiggle animation.\nI just saw an extremely compelling demo of using wrist rotation as an extremely precise mouse pointer that allows the presenter to position their hand as they choose. 3dof rotation is actually far more precise than the thumbstick or hand position... However, the example I saw was done in SDK2 using a shader. It would be really cool to have the same capability directly through the animator rather than requiring a custom shader.\nThe canny here is specifically about driving a puppet using wrist rotation, rather than a specific task.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
